;; Docs for samizdat/apidocs.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "apidocs.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}. A command name
;; in code is written @code|{@name}|, so that it is not run.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Commands that show the docs of a Bjolang module in a document.")
  (see generate-docs defmodule defdoc defdocs docref)
  (example """
    (meta (title "(std random)"))

    @generate-docs[#:module "std random"]{
      Text of your own, at the end of the overview.
    }

    @comment{Or arrange the page yourself:}

    @defmodule[(std random)]{
      Everything here takes @code{#:rng}.
    }

    @defdoc[random-int]{
      A die is @code{(random-int 1 6)}.
    }

    Or use @docref[random-double], further down.

    @defdoc[random-double]
    """)
  (reference """
    The docs are the @code{(:doc ...)} forms that the compiler published in
    the dll of the module. The commands read them with @code{(bjo docs)}
    while the document is expanded. So a page shows the docs of the module
    as it was compiled. The dll is added to the @code{sources} of the
    document, so a kept page is stale when the module is built again.

    @strong{Which module.} A module is written in one of two ways:

    @itemlist{
    @item{A name, such as @code{(std random)}. It is found as the compiler finds it: in the installed library, and in the packages of the nearest @code{.bjo/roots} above the document.}
    @item{A string, the path of the source of the module, relative to the document. The dll must be beside the source.}
    }

    @strong{Texts.} The texts in a doc are samizdat markup. They are
    expanded with the commands of the document.

    @strong{Errors.} These are rejected with @code{refuse}: a module that is
    not found, a module that is not compiled, a name that the module has no
    doc for, a definition that the document shows twice, and a
    @code|{@docref}| to a definition that the document does not show.

    @strong{Classes.} An entry is a @code{Div} of class @code{doc-entry}.
    The doc of a module is a @code{Div} of class @code{doc-module}. The kind
    of an entry is a @code{Span} of class @code{doc-kind}. On a page that
    @code|{@generate-docs}| made, the link up to the index is a @code{Span}
    of class @code{doc-up}, and the index is a @code{Div} of class
    @code{doc-index}.
    """))

(:doc generate-docs
  (summary "A command that makes a whole page for the docs of a module.")
  (arg cx "The state of the document.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "The blocks of the page.")
  (see defmodule defdocs docref)
  (example """
    @generate-docs[#:module "std datetime"]
    @generate-docs[#:path "src/thing.bjo"]{Text for the overview.}
    """)
  (reference """
    It takes @code{#:module} or @code{#:path}, not both. @code{#:module} is
    the name of the module, as a string with or without parentheses:
    @code{"std datetime"}. @code{#:path} is the path of the source of the
    module, relative to the document.

    The page has these parts:

    @itemlist{
    @item{A link to the reference.}
    @item{A heading @em{Overview}, then the doc of the module, then the body.}
    @item{A heading @em{Reference}, then an index. The index has a group for each kind: types, functions, values, macros. In each group, the names are in alphabetical order. Each name is a link to its entry.}
    @item{A heading for each group, then the entry of each definition of that kind, in the order of the module. Each entry has a link back to its group in the index.}
    }

    The headings of the parts are at level 1, the groups at level 2, and
    the entries at level 3. After the command, the module is the module of
    @code|{@defdoc}|, @code|{@defdocs}| and @code|{@docref}|.

    Positional arguments, a call with neither keyword, and a call with both
    are rejected with @code{refuse}.
    """))

(:doc defmodule
  (summary "A command that shows the doc of a module, and sets the module of the commands after it.")
  (arg cx "The state of the document.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "A @code{Div} of class @code{doc-module}: the doc of the module, then the body.")
  (see defdoc defdocs docref generate-docs)
  (example """
    @defmodule[(std random)]{
      Everything here takes @code{#:rng}.
    }
    @defmodule["src/thing.bjo"]
    """)
  (reference """
    It takes one positional argument: the name of the module, or the path
    of its source as a string. It takes no keywords. A module with no
    module doc shows only the body.

    @code|{@defdoc}|, @code|{@defdocs}| and @code|{@docref}| without
    @code{#:module} use this module, until the next @code|{@defmodule}| or
    @code|{@generate-docs}|.
    """))

(:doc defdoc
  (summary "A command that shows the doc of one definition, then the body.")
  (arg cx "The state of the document.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "A @code{Div} of class @code{doc-entry}.")
  (see defdocs defmodule docref)
  (example """
    @defdoc[random-int]{
      A die is @code{(random-int 1 6)}.
    }
    @defdoc["#pair"]
    @defdoc[random-double #:module (std random) #:level 3]
    """)
  (reference """
    The argument is the name of the definition. A reader extension is named
    with a string: @code{"#pair"}. @code{#:module} names a module other than
    the module of @code|{@defmodule}|. @code{#:level} is the level of the
    heading, 4 by default, so that a table of contents of sections does not
    list the definitions.

    The entry has:

    @itemlist{
    @item{a heading with the name, which a link can go to;}
    @item{the kind of the definition;}
    @item{the type and the head of the definition, or the forms of a macro;}
    @item{the summary;}
    @item{the arguments, the keywords, the fields, the cases, the type parameters, the result and the exceptions, with their texts;}
    @item{the examples, the reference text, and links to the related names that the document shows.}
    }

    A call with no name, a module that no @code|{@defmodule}| or
    @code{#:module} gives, and a name that is shown already are rejected
    with @code{refuse}.
    """))

(:doc defdocs
  (summary "A command that shows the docs of every definition of a module, in the order of the module.")
  (arg cx "The state of the document.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "One @code{Div} of class @code{doc-entry} for each definition. The doc of the module is not included.")
  (see defdoc defmodule)
  (example """
    @defmodule[(std random)]
    @defdocs
    @defdocs[#:module (std datetime) #:level 3]
    """)
  (reference """
    It takes @code{#:module} and @code{#:level}, as @code|{@defdoc}| does.
    Positional arguments and a body are rejected with @code{refuse}. Use
    @code|{@defdoc}| for a definition with text of your own.
    """))

(:doc docref
  (summary "A command that makes a link to a definition that the document shows, before or after the link.")
  (arg cx "The state of the document.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "A link to the entry. Its text is the body, or the name as code when there is no body.")
  (see defdoc defdocs generate-docs)
  (example """
    See @docref[random-int].
    See @docref[random-int]{the function for dice}.
    """)
  (reference """
    It takes @code{#:module}, as @code|{@defdoc}| does. A name that the
    module has no doc for is rejected with @code{refuse}. A name that the
    document does not show with @code|{@defdoc}|, @code|{@defdocs}| or
    @code|{@generate-docs}| is also rejected, after every command ran.
    """))
